Add the LikeOS native and target-dependent modules.

Two new files, both small, because almost everything a debugger needs on this
system is already in gdb.

gdb/likeos-nat.c is the native side.  Attach, detach, resume, step, wait, read
and write memory, kill -- inf-ptrace.c does all of it unchanged, since LikeOS's
ptrace(2) speaks the same requests with the same arguments.  What is genuinely
local is the shape of the register block, so that is all this file holds: a
table saying where each register gdb knows about sits inside `struct
ptrace_regs', and the two methods that move a block in and out.  Both register
orders are written out in the comment rather than left to be counted off,
because an off-by-one there does not crash -- it silently reports one register's
value under another's name.  The floating-point block needs no translation at
all: the kernel reports an FXSAVE image and amd64_supply_fxsave expects one.

gdb/likeos-tdep.c registers the OS ABI, and its one real line points the
architecture at solib-svr4.  The loader fills in DT_DEBUG and maintains the
link_map chain, calling _dl_debug_state before and after each change so a
debugger can tell a finished list from one mid-edit; that is the protocol
solib-svr4 implements, so naming it is the whole of the work.

Both use INIT_GDB_FILE rather than a hand-written _initialize_ function.  That
is not a style preference: gdb builds its table of initialisers by grepping the
sources for that macro (see make-init-c), so a plain _initialize_likeos_tdep
would compile, link, and never be called -- leaving the registration undone and
shared libraries invisible, with nothing to indicate why.

Two things this file carries beyond the register table, both learned from a
first run on the machine:

  - xfer_partial serves TARGET_OBJECT_AUXV out of the new PTRACE_GETAUXV.  This
    is what makes breakpoints land.  Executables here are position-independent,
    and without the auxiliary vector gdb has no way to learn the bias one was
    loaded at: `break main' resolved to its link-time 0x590, the breakpoint
    could not be written because nothing is mapped there, and every run ended
    in "Cannot access memory at address 0x590".  solib-svr4 takes AT_ENTRY from
    this vector, subtracts e_entry from the file on disk, and relocates the
    symbol table by the difference.  A system with a per-process filesystem
    publishes the same bytes as a file and needs no code for it; this one asks
    the kernel directly.

  - The register offset table runs to %gs_base rather than stopping at %gs.
    The table is indexed by gdb's own register number and %fs_base is number
    153, so the entire x87/SSE/AVX/AVX512 file has to be spelled out as absent
    in between.  Stopping short left both segment bases reported as
    "<unavailable>", and %fs_base is where a thread's TLS block lives, so
    thread-local variables were unreachable.  A static_assert ties the table's
    length to AMD64_GSBASE_REGNUM, because getting that run of -1 wrong does
    not fail loudly -- it slides the last two entries onto some other
    register's offset.

likeos-tdep.c registers an OS ABI *sniffer* as well as a handler, and without
the sniffer the handler is dead code.  LikeOS binaries are plain ELF -- EI_OSABI
is ELFOSABI_NONE and there is no OS note -- so gdb's generic sniffer finds no ABI
tag and reports GDB_OSABI_UNKNOWN.  An unknown OS ABI means likeos_init_abi is
never called and everything it sets up silently does not happen: no solib ops,
so no shared library list, and no relocation of a position-independent
executable.  That is what left `break main' resolving to its link-time 0x590,
every frame printing as "?? ()", and the register fetch working perfectly well
all the while -- the native side is registered unconditionally, the target side
is gated on the OS ABI.  Claiming every unmarked ELF is safe in a gdb configured
for this target alone; systems that stamp their binaries are still matched
first, since their EI_OSABI is not ELFOSABI_NONE.

likeos_init_abi calls amd64_init_abi before anything else, which every other
amd64 OS handler in the tree also does and which is not a formality: it installs
the frame unwinders, the calling convention, the register set and the breakpoint
kind.  Registering the OS ABI without it made the debugger worse than having no
OS ABI at all -- gdb had been falling back to a default amd64 architecture that
carried all of those, and claiming the ABI replaced that fallback with a handler
that only set solib ops.  The result did not look like a missing initialiser:
registers still printed, because the native target reads those itself, and the
program still ran, but the first frame gdb unwound ended the session with
"frame-unwind.c:231: internal-error: frame_unwind_find_by_frame failed" and
breakpoints quietly failed to land.

likeos-nat.c also enumerates the inferior's THREADS, through PT_GETNUMLWPS and
PT_GETLWPLIST.  A thread on this system is a task in its own right -- its own
registers, its own stop, its own id -- so ptrace already works on one once its
id is known; knowing the SET is the only thing a debugger cannot work out for
itself, since a thread is invisible from outside the process.  Without this gdb
reports a single thread, which for a program that does its work on several
means one stack out of five and a fault on any other thread attributed to the
wrong one.  update_thread_list prunes and re-adds on every stop, because a
thread can both appear and exit between two of them; the first thread reported
renames the existing inferior ptid rather than being added beside it, or the
process shows up twice.

Naming threads means overriding wait() as well, and the two halves are not
separable.  Once the thread list holds (process, thread) ptids, everything
handed to gdb must carry both: a stop reported as (process, 0) matches no
thread, gdb looks for the inferior that owns it, finds none, and dies in
set_current_inferior on `inf != NULL'.  The reference targets do this from the
other side -- FreeBSD's wait_1 takes the process id wait() gave it and asks
PT_LWPINFO which LWP stopped -- whereas this kernel reports the TASK that
stopped, so the thread half arrives for free and the process half is the
inferior being waited on.

The rest of what a native target owes gdb is here too, and the pieces are not
separable from each other:

  - wait() reports (process, thread).  Once the thread list holds such ptids,
    everything handed to gdb must carry both halves, or a stop matches no
    thread and set_current_inferior dies on `inf != NULL'.  FreeBSD does this
    from the other side, asking PT_LWPINFO which LWP stopped; this kernel
    reports the TASK that stopped, so the thread half arrives for free.
  - resume() resumes the PROCESS unless a single thread was named or the
    request is a step.  The kernel parks every thread when one stops, so
    releasing only the named thread would leave the siblings parked with
    nothing left to release them.  A step stays aimed at one thread, because
    letting the others run during it lands the step somewhere unrelated.
  - The event stops are decoded: fork, vfork, clone, exec and thread exit all
    arrive as SIGTRAP and are indistinguishable from a breakpoint without
    asking for the event code (PTRACE_GETEVENT) and the pid that goes with it
    (PTRACE_GETEVENTMSG).  The clone and exit stops are consumed here rather
    than reported: they exist so a thread can be added the moment it is born
    and dropped the moment it dies, which is what makes `info threads' current
    between stops instead of only at them.
  - sharing_input_terminal() in posix-hdep.c answers with the session id.  It
    returned "unknown" here, and the caller turns that into "assume yes", so
    attaching to a process on another terminal handed the foreground group of
    GDB's OWN terminal to a group living somewhere else -- ^C then went there
    instead of to GDB, and `gdb -p <an xterm>' delivered SIGINT to the whole X
    session.  A process in a different session cannot have this terminal as its
    controlling terminal, which is the case that matters and the one thing that
    can be answered without a per-process filesystem.
  - pass_signal() in inflow.c names the inferior through the INFERIOR.  It used
    inferior_ptid, which the wait path deliberately clears before calling the
    target (do_target_wait_1 starts with switch_to_inferior_no_thread), so ^C
    ran `kill (0, SIGINT)' -- every process in GDB's OWN process group, not the
    program.  Only targets that block in the wait install this handler, which is
    why it survives upstream: it shows as ^C working for a program GDB started
    and doing nothing for one GDB attached to.
  - attach() asks for those events too.  post_startup_inferior is reached only
    from create_inferior, so a process gdb ATTACHED to was traced with no
    options at all -- no thread announced, no thread reported gone, an exec
    swapping the program out in silence.
  - $_siginfo, through TARGET_OBJECT_SIGNAL_INFO and PTRACE_GETSIGINFO: si_code
    and si_addr are the difference between "SIGSEGV" and "wrote to 0x1".
  - %ds, %es, %fs and %gs are read from the register block instead of being
    marked absent.  The kernel now reports them -- captured on entry from user
    mode, since a stopped tracee's segment registers are not in the processor
    any more -- so gdb can print them.  They are appended after the two segment
    bases at the end of `struct ptrace_regs', which is why the four offsets are
    22..25 rather than beside %cs and %ss: adding them there would have moved
    every register after them.

diff -urN a/gdb/inflow.c b/gdb/inflow.c
--- a/gdb/inflow.c
+++ b/gdb/inflow.c
@@ -899,7 +899,30 @@
 pass_signal (int signo)
 {
 #ifndef _WIN32
-  kill (inferior_ptid.pid (), SIGINT);
+  /* Name the inferior through the INFERIOR, not through inferior_ptid.
+
+     This handler is installed around the blocking wait, and the wait path
+     clears inferior_ptid before calling the target: do_target_wait_1 begins
+     with switch_to_inferior_no_thread, whose whole purpose is to make sure
+     nothing in the wait code relies on inferior_ptid.  So by the time ^C
+     arrives it is null_ptid, and `kill (0, SIGINT)' does not mean "the
+     inferior" -- POSIX defines it as every process in the CALLER's process
+     group, which is GDB.  ^C then never reached the program at all; it went
+     back into this handler.
+
+     Only targets that block in the wait see it, which is why it survives: the
+     async targets interrupt through target_interrupt() and never install this
+     handler.  It shows up as ^C working for a program GDB started (that one
+     shares GDB's terminal, so the tty delivers SIGINT to it directly and
+     set_sigint_trap installs nothing) and doing nothing at all for a program
+     GDB attached to.  */
+  inferior *inf = current_inferior ();
+  int pid = (inferior_ptid != null_ptid
+	     ? inferior_ptid.pid ()
+	     : (inf != nullptr ? inf->pid : 0));
+
+  if (pid > 0)
+    kill (pid, SIGINT);
 #endif
 }
 
diff -urN a/gdb/likeos-nat.c b/gdb/likeos-nat.c
--- a/gdb/likeos-nat.c
+++ b/gdb/likeos-nat.c
@@ -0,0 +1,947 @@
+/* Native-dependent code for LikeOS/amd64.
+
+   This file is part of GDB.
+
